// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  THE NEGATIVE CONTROL — a predicate no result satisfies. The search exhausts and everything
//  rests.
//
//  Run me with the RhoCalc interpreter:
//    target/debug/rholang demos/flt-assay-desk/desk-accepts-nothing.rho
//
//  Same three resting results, same pattern, same channel. The only change is the term the
//  `where` clause compares against: the THREE-argument constant combinator
//        lam a. lam b. lam c. a       (= λ.λ.λ.2)
//  which is not the normal form of any of the three contracts.
//
//  WHY THIS BEAT EXISTS
//  ────────────────────
//  Without it, "the desk accepted exactly one" is compatible with a broken guard that happens
//  to admit the first datum it is handed. With it, the three outcomes are separated:
//
//        desk-accepts-constant.rho   selects ⟦λ.λ.1⟧      the guard admits exactly one
//        desk-accepts-identity.rho   selects ⟦λ.0⟧        a DIFFERENT one, same set
//        desk-accepts-nothing.rho    selects nothing      the guard can refuse ALL of them
//
//  No single defect produces all three. A vacuous guard would settle something in all three; a
//  fail-closed guard would settle nothing in all three; a guard that stops at the first datum
//  could not have produced two different answers from one set.
//
//  EXPECT
//  ──────
//        @"OUT": (the program rested without publishing any observation)
//        all three results still resting on @"assay"
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@"assay"!(lambda`lam x. x`) |
@"assay"!(lambda`lam a. lam b. b`) |
@"assay"!(lambda`lam a. lam b. a`) |

for(@lambda`${r}` <- @"assay" where lambda`${r}` == lambda`lam a. lam b. lam c. a`) {
  @"OUT"!(lambda`${r}`)
}
